<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ichijōdani</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ichijodani</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ichijōdani is a narrow valley near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fukui">Fukui</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, where the Asakura clan built their castle and town in the late 15th century. Under five generations of Asakura rulers it became a prosperous cultural centre that welcomed refugee courtiers and scholars from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oda-nobunaga">Oda Nobunaga</a> burned it in 1573, and the excavated ruins, with restored streets and gardens, are a protected historic site.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ichijodani.jpg" alt="Ichijōdani" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 663highland · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Asakura_Yakata_of_Ichijodani_Asakura_Family_Historic_Ruins02s3s4440.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
